    <h1 class="section-title">What Is Ethical Hacking?</h1>

    <div class="intro-block">
      Ethical hacking is the authorized practice of intentionally probing computer systems, networks, and applications for security weaknesses — before a malicious actor can find and exploit them. An ethical hacker uses the same techniques and tools as any other attacker. The single thing that sets them apart is <strong style="color:var(--green)">permission</strong>.
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Permission Is Everything</h3>
      <p>Illegal hacking and ethical hacking are technically identical. Same tools. Same methods. Same vulnerabilities. The difference is a signed authorization document. Without written permission from the system owner, probing a network is a federal crime under the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA). With it, the exact same action is a professional service. Intent matters — but intent without authorization is not a legal defense.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Why Organizations Hire Ethical Hackers</h3>
      <p>Organizations cannot defend against attacks they haven't tested for. Ethical hackers — also called penetration testers or "pentesters" — are hired to think like attackers and find weaknesses in a controlled way. They deliver findings in a report that includes the vulnerabilities discovered, the risk they represent, and concrete recommendations to fix them. The goal is always to improve the organization's security posture before a real attacker gets the chance.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// The Hacker Mindset</h3>
      <p>Real security comes from thinking adversarially. Ethical hackers ask: "How would someone break this?" They probe assumptions, test edge cases, and look for misconfigurations that slip past normal audits. Their value isn't just in technical skill — it's in bringing an outsider's creative curiosity to find what defenders have become blind to.</p>
    </div>

