<div class="page" id="page-cli">
  <div class="content-wrap">
    <div class="section-tag">// 01 — TOOL</div>
    <h1 class="section-title">CLI — Command-Line Interface</h1>

    <div class="intro-block">
      The Command-Line Interface is a text-based method of interacting with a computer's operating system by typing commands. Instead of clicking icons, you type instructions directly and receive text output in return. It's the foundation of nearly everything a security professional does.
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// CLI vs GUI</h3>
      <p>A Graphical User Interface (GUI) lets you interact with visual elements — windows, buttons, and menus. A CLI removes all of that and communicates directly with the OS kernel through text. GUIs are easier for casual users but GUI applications only expose the features developers decided to show. The CLI exposes <em>everything</em>. For a security tester, that total control matters.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Why Security Professionals Live in the Terminal</h3>
      <p><strong style="color:var(--green)">Speed:</strong> Experienced users execute complex operations in seconds that would take minutes clicking through menus.<br><br>
      <strong style="color:var(--green)">Precision:</strong> Commands are exact. There's no ambiguity about what you're doing — unlike GUI apps that hide implementation details.<br><br>
      <strong style="color:var(--green)">Scripting:</strong> CLI commands can be chained and scripted. Automating repetitive tasks (scanning a subnet, parsing output) is only practical from the terminal.<br><br>
      <strong style="color:var(--green)">Remote Access:</strong> SSH sessions are terminal-only. When you're connected to a remote machine, there's often no GUI at all.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Real Lab Commands</h3>
      <div class="code-block" data-label="BASH">
