<!-- ══════════════════ PAGE: SSH ══════════════════ -->
<div class="page" id="page-ssh">
  <div class="content-wrap">
    <div class="section-tag">// 02 — TOOL</div>
    <h1 class="section-title">SSH — Secure Shell</h1>

    <div class="intro-block">
      SSH (Secure Shell) is a cryptographic network protocol that lets you operate a remote computer's command line over an encrypted connection — as if you were sitting in front of it. Before SSH, tools like Telnet sent all traffic, including passwords, in plain text across the network. Anyone with a packet sniffer could read it. SSH eliminated that problem entirely.
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// The Problem SSH Solved</h3>
      <p>In the early days of networking, administrators used Telnet to remotely manage systems. Telnet worked, but it had a fatal flaw: it transmitted everything — commands, responses, and credentials — as unencrypted plain text. SSH replaced it with an encrypted tunnel using public-key cryptography, making interception of the connection useless. The attacker might capture the traffic, but what they'd see would be cipher gibberish.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// What You Can Do Over SSH</h3>
      <p>Once connected, you have full shell access to the remote machine. In a penetration testing context this means: running reconnaissance commands, reading files and logs, checking for privilege escalation paths, installing or removing software, pivoting to other machines on the internal network, and executing anything you'd do if you were physically present — quietly and from anywhere in the world.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Command Breakdown — Lab Example</h3>
      <div class="code-block" data-label="TERMINAL">
<span class="comment"># Connect to the Raspberry Pi found during the Nmap lab</span>
ssh student@192.168.1.100

<span class="comment"># Breaking down each part:</span>
<span class="flag">ssh</span>           <span class="comment">→ the SSH client program</span>
<span class="flag">student</span>       <span class="comment">→ the username to log in as on the remote machine</span>
<span class="flag">@</span>             <span class="comment">→ separator between username and host</span>
<span class="flag">192.168.1.100</span> <span class="comment">→ the IP address of the target (found via Nmap)</span>

<span class="output">The authenticity of host '192.168.1.100' can't be established.
ED25519 key fingerprint is SHA256:xyz...
Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no)? yes
student@192.168.1.100's password:
Linux raspberrypi 5.15.0 #1 SMP armv7l GNU/Linux
student@raspberrypi:~$</span>
      </div>
      <p>In the grade-change lab, the workflow was: scan the network with Nmap to discover the Raspberry Pi → identify port 22 open (SSH) → connect with <code style="color:var(--green)">ssh student@&lt;ip&gt;</code> → once inside, navigate to student records and demonstrate what a real attacker could do. This is the chain that penetration testing exposes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <footer>0xKNOWLEDGE &nbsp;|&nbsp; Ethical Hacking Reference</footer>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ PAGE: NMAP ══════════════════ -->
<div class="page" id="page-nmap">
  <div class="content-wrap">
    <div class="section-tag">// 03 — TOOL</div>
    <h1 class="section-title">Nmap — Network Mapper</h1>

    <div class="intro-block">
      Nmap is an open-source tool for network discovery and security auditing. It sends carefully crafted packets to hosts on a network and analyzes the responses to determine which systems are online, which ports are open, and what services — and versions of those services — are running on them.
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// What Are Ports?</h3>
      <p>A port is a numbered endpoint on a networked computer — think of it like a slot on the side of a building where specific kinds of traffic enter and exit. Port 22 is conventionally used for SSH. Port 80 for HTTP web traffic. Port 443 for HTTPS. Port 3306 for MySQL databases. When a service is "running on a port," it means it's actively listening for connections on that number. To a security tester, an open port is a potential entry point. Knowing what's open — and what version is running — is the starting point for almost every test.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Nmap Command — With Real Output</h3>
      <div class="code-block" data-label="NMAP SCAN">
<span class="comment"># Scan an entire subnet and detect service versions</span>
nmap -sV 192.168.1.0/24

<span class="flag">-sV</span>          <span class="comment">→ version detection — identifies what software is on each open port</span>
<span class="flag">192.168.1.0/24</span> <span class="comment">→ scan all 254 hosts on this subnet (CIDR notation)</span>

<span class="comment"># Sample output:</span>
<span class="output">Nmap scan report for 192.168.1.100
PORT     STATE  SERVICE  VERSION
22/tcp   open   ssh      OpenSSH 7.9
80/tcp   open   http     Apache httpd 2.4.38
3306/tcp open   mysql    MySQL 5.7.36
8080/tcp open   http-alt Werkzeug httpd 1.0.1</span>

<span class="comment"># Port 3306 open = a database directly exposed on the local network.</span>
<span class="comment"># Port 8080 running Werkzeug = likely a development server (not hardened).</span>
<span class="comment"># Both are findings worth documenting in a pentest report.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

