<!-- ══════════════════ PAGE: WIRESHARK ══════════════════ -->
<div class="page" id="page-wireshark">
  <div class="content-wrap">
    <div class="section-tag">// 04 — TOOL</div>
    <h1 class="section-title">Wireshark — Packet Analyzer</h1>

    <div class="intro-block">
      Wireshark is a free, open-source network protocol analyzer that captures and lets you inspect every packet traveling across a network interface in real time. It's one of the most widely used tools in both security research and network troubleshooting.
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// What Is a Packet?</h3>
      <p>Think of a packet like a physical letter traveling through the postal system. The letter has an envelope with a "from" address (source IP) and a "to" address (destination IP), and inside the envelope is the actual content — the payload. Network communication works the same way: data is broken into small chunks called packets, each with header information describing where it's going and where it came from, plus the actual data being transmitted. Wireshark lets you open every envelope and read every letter passing through your network interface.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// What Wireshark Reveals</h3>
      <p>Wireshark doesn't use command-line syntax the way Nmap does — it's a GUI application that displays a live feed of packets. During a security test, Wireshark can expose: unencrypted credentials in HTTP login forms, DNS queries revealing internal hostnames, ARP traffic mapping the network's physical-to-IP address table, and any data transmitted in plain text over the network. The key insight is that anything unencrypted is readable to anyone watching the traffic.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Real-World Example: HTTP Login Capture</h3>
      <p>Imagine a user on the same network submits a login form to a site running HTTP (not HTTPS). Wireshark captures that traffic and reveals the raw POST request, including the username and password in plain text. Below is what that captured exchange looks like:</p>
      <table class="packet-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>#</th><th>Source</th><th>Destination</th><th>Protocol</th><th>Info</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>192.168.1.55</td><td>203.0.113.10</td><td>TCP</td><td>SYN → port 80</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>203.0.113.10</td><td>192.168.1.55</td><td>TCP</td><td>SYN-ACK</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>192.168.1.55</td><td>203.0.113.10</td><td>HTTP</td><td>POST /login.php HTTP/1.1</td></tr>
          <tr><td></td><td colspan="4" class="sensitive">Payload: username=admin&password=hunter2 [PLAIN TEXT — VISIBLE]</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>203.0.113.10</td><td>192.168.1.55</td><td>HTTP</td><td>HTTP/1.1 302 Found → redirect to /dashboard</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem">In a real test, this finding would be reported as: <em>"Credentials transmitted over unencrypted HTTP. Intercepted via passive network capture. Remediation: enforce HTTPS site-wide using TLS 1.2 or higher."</em></p>
    </div>

    <div class="ethics-block">
      <h3>⚠ LEGAL BOUNDARY</h3>
      <p>Capturing network traffic you don't own or don't have authorization to monitor is illegal under the Electronic Communications Privacy Act (ECPA). Wireshark is a legitimate diagnostic and security tool — on networks where you have permission. Passive sniffing on a network without consent is federal wire-tapping.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <footer>0xKNOWLEDGE &nbsp;|&nbsp; Ethical Hacking Reference</footer>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ PAGE: HYDRA ══════════════════ -->
<div class="page" id="page-hydra">
  <div class="content-wrap">
    <div class="section-tag">// 05 — TOOL</div>
    <h1 class="section-title">Hydra — Credential Testing Tool</h1>

    <div class="intro-block">
      THC-Hydra is a fast, parallelized login-cracking tool used by authorized security testers to verify whether systems accept weak or commonly used passwords. It automates the process of submitting credential combinations against login interfaces — including SSH, FTP, HTTP forms, and many others.
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// What Hydra Does and Why Testers Use It</h3>
      <p>When an organization asks a pentester to assess their authentication systems, one critical question is: "Do any accounts use weak or default passwords?" Manually trying thousands of passwords is not feasible. Hydra automates this by taking a list of passwords and rapidly submitting them against a login service. If any password in the list succeeds, Hydra reports it. A security tester uses this to confirm the vulnerability, then documents it in a report so the organization can reset the credential and improve their password policy.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// What Is a Wordlist?</h3>
      <p>A wordlist is simply a text file containing one password per line — typically compiled from data breaches, research into common password patterns, and keyboard walks. The most well-known wordlist is <code style="color:var(--green)">rockyou.txt</code>, which contains over 14 million real passwords leaked from a 2009 data breach. Hydra reads through the wordlist sequentially, submitting each password against the target service until one works or the list is exhausted.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Lab Command — Full Flag Breakdown</h3>
      <div class="code-block" data-label="HYDRA">
<span class="comment"># Authorized credential test against SSH service on 192.168.1.50</span>
hydra -l admin -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt ssh://192.168.1.50

<span class="flag">hydra</span>             <span class="comment">→ the program being called</span>
<span class="flag">-l admin</span>         <span class="comment">→ lowercase -l: a single, specific username to test</span>
<span class="flag">-P rockyou.txt</span>  <span class="comment">→ uppercase -P: a file path to the password wordlist</span>
<span class="flag">ssh://</span>           <span class="comment">→ the protocol to attack (Hydra supports dozens)</span>
<span class="flag">192.168.1.50</span>    <span class="comment">→ the IP address of the target SSH service</span>

<span class="comment"># Output when a credential is found:</span>
<span class="found">[22][ssh] host: 192.168.1.50   login: admin   password: password1</span>

<span class="comment"># Finding: Account 'admin' uses the password 'password1'.</span>
<span class="comment"># Action: Report it. Recommend forced reset and MFA enforcement.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ethics-block">
      <h3>⚠ ETHICS — REQUIRED READING</h3>
      <p><strong>Running Hydra against any system without written authorization is illegal.</strong> It doesn't matter if the system appears to be abandoned, misconfigured, or "obviously vulnerable." Unauthorized credential testing violates the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA) — a federal statute with penalties including prison time and significant fines. The authorization document signed before a penetration test is what separates a security professional from a criminal using the exact same tool.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="defense-block">
      <h3>// DEFENSES AGAINST CREDENTIAL ATTACKS</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Account Lockout Policies:</strong> Automatically lock an account after a set number of failed attempts (e.g., 5). This forces an attacker to slow down dramatically or abandon the attack entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Rate Limiting:</strong> Limit how many login attempts can come from a single IP per minute. Hydra's speed is its strength — rate limiting removes that advantage.</li>
        <li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> Even if Hydra finds the correct password, MFA means the attacker still can't log in without a second factor (a phone code, hardware key, etc.). This is the most impactful defense.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <footer>0xKNOWLEDGE &nbsp;|&nbsp; Ethical Hacking Reference</footer>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ PAGE: BRUTE FORCE ══════════════════ -->
<div class="page" id="page-bruteforce">
  <div class="content-wrap">
    <div class="section-tag">// 06 — CONCEPT</div>
    <h1 class="section-title">Brute-Force Attacks</h1>

    <div class="intro-block">
      Brute-force is an attack <em>method</em>, not a single tool. It describes the strategy of systematically trying every possible combination of credentials, characters, or keys until the correct one is found. It's the computational equivalent of trying every key on a keyring, one by one, until a door opens.
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Brute-Force vs. Dictionary Attack</h3>
      <p><strong style="color:var(--green)">Brute-Force Attack:</strong> Tries every possible combination in a defined character set — every letter, number, and symbol in every possible order. If the password space is small enough, this will eventually succeed. The downside is time: an 8-character password with mixed case and symbols has over 200 trillion combinations. Even modern hardware can take years.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:0.8rem"><strong style="color:var(--green)">Dictionary Attack:</strong> Works smarter. Instead of every possible combination, it uses a pre-built wordlist of known passwords, common words, leaked credentials, and predictable patterns. Most real users pick passwords that appear in wordlists, making dictionary attacks far faster and more practical in real engagements. Hydra's <code style="color:var(--green)">rockyou.txt</code> attack is a dictionary attack.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>// Penetration Test Scenario</h3>
      <p>During an authorized test, a tester identifies an SSH service on a server with no account lockout policy. They run Hydra with the rockyou.txt wordlist against the <code style="color:var(--green)">admin</code> account. Within minutes, Hydra returns a valid credential. The tester logs in, documents the access level achieved, and photographs proof. The final report reads:</p>
      <div class="code-block" data-label="PENTEST FINDING">
<span class="comment">FINDING: Weak Credentials + No Lockout Policy</span>
<span class="output">Severity:    Critical
Service:     SSH (Port 22)
Host:        192.168.1.50
Credential:  admin / password1
Method:      Dictionary attack (rockyou.txt) — succeeded in 4 minutes</span>

<span class="comment">RISK: Full unauthorized shell access to production server.</span>
<span class="comment">RECOMMENDATION:</span>
<span class="output">1. Immediately reset the 'admin' credential.
2. Enforce a minimum 12-character passphrase policy.
3. Implement account lockout after 5 failed attempts.
4. Require MFA for all SSH access.
5. Disable password auth entirely — enforce SSH key pairs.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ethics-block">
      <h3>⚠ ETHICS — THREE DEFENSES EXPLAINED</h3>
      <p><strong>1. Account Lockout:</strong> After a defined number of failed logins, the account is disabled for a period or until unlocked by an admin. This turns a dictionary attack from a 4-minute exercise into an infeasible multi-year project.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:0.6rem"><strong>2. Rate Limiting:</strong> Systems can detect abnormally high login request rates and throttle or block them. A human trying passwords manually might make 5 attempts per minute. Hydra can make thousands. Rate limiting detects and kills that pattern.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:0.6rem"><strong>3. Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> Even a perfectly cracked password becomes worthless if the system also requires a one-time code from an authenticator app or hardware token. MFA makes stolen or guessed passwords essentially useless on their own.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- HYDRA COMMAND BUILDER BONUS -->
